  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Saturday)...\n\nThe record breaking hot temperatures yesterday (highs in the \nupper 90s to 100s) will ramp up further today, as strong high \npressure and warm temperatures aloft continue to build over the \nregion. Expect high temperatures to make it into the 100s, with \nsome locations exceeding 110 degrees. Lower valley locations east\nof Billings continue to see the best chance at eclipsing 110. \nAll-time record highs will be threatened at all climate sites \ntoday. Warm temperatures will persist into tonight, with lows in \nthe 60s west to the 70s to near 80 degrees for central and eastern\nlocations. This will continue to exacerbate the risk of heat \nstress. A weak front will sweep into the area from the \nwest/northwest this evening. This will cool temps 5 to 10 degrees \nfor Monday (relative to today), but highs will still be in the \nupper 90s to around 100 degrees. An Extreme Heat Warning remains \nin effect through Monday.\n\nElevated to critical fire weather concerns can be expected for \nmost locations today due to the combination of hot temperatures, \nvery dry conditions (afternoon relative humidity down into the \nsingle digits and teens percent) and breezy winds. Expect breezy \nsouth to southwest winds to develop over the area (gusts 20s to \n30s mph) today, ahead of the aforementioned cold front this \nevening/night. While a small chance (10% or less), some hi-res\nguidance indicate a few high-based showers/virga and perhaps a\ndry thunderstorm could move north over western areas late this \nafternoon and evening. Very warm mid-level temperatures typically\ninhibit convective activity, but it is something to monitor. A \nRed Flag Warning is in effect from 9am this morning through 9pm \nthis evening for most areas. Please take care not to cause a spark\nor do any burning.\n\nTemperatures trend a little cooler heading into mid week as the \nridge axis and highest heights shift east into the northern \nplains. Highs in the upper 80s and 90s are forecast through the \nremainder of the week. Models indicate monsoonal moisture will \nslide into our region from the southwest with hints of occasional \nweak perturbations in the mean flow. At a minimum, this looks to \nbring an increase in relative humidity, with moderate chances at \ndaily showers/thunderstorms for the area. Ensembles continue to \nsuggest remarkable PWATs possibly reaching up to 1.50 inches in \nthe area from Tuesday onward. So any decent impulse in the \nmonsoonal flow may result in storms with locally heavy rainfall. \nWe will need to monitor those PWAT trends closely heading into \nnext week. \n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F\n\nSTP",
